A maker adds this formula to a button in a canvas app to submit an expense workflow.

Snippet

UpdateContext({busy:true});
ExpenseSubmit.Run(txtExpenseId.Text);
Notify("Submitted");
UpdateContext({busy:false});
Which characteristic applies to this design?

Correct answer
A. Async button-triggered flow

Explanation
This design is characteristic of an Async button-triggered flow because the button triggers the execution of the ExpenseSubmit flow asynchronously. The UpdateContext functions are used to set and reset the 'busy' flag, which indicates that the flow is running in the background while the user is notified that the expense has been submitted.
Your answer is incorrect
B. In-transaction server logic

Explanation
This design does not exhibit characteristics of in-transaction server logic. In-transaction server logic typically involves handling multiple operations within a single transaction to ensure data integrity and consistency, which is not explicitly demonstrated in this scenario.
C. Triggerless app reference model

Explanation
This design does not align with the triggerless app reference model. A triggerless app reference model typically refers to apps that do not rely on external triggers or events to initiate actions. In this case, the button click serves as the trigger for the expense submission flow.
D. Label Text invocation pattern

Explanation
This design does not follow the Label Text invocation pattern. The Label Text invocation pattern typically involves using labels or text elements to trigger actions or display information in the app interface. In this scenario, the button is used to trigger the expense submission flow, not a label or text element.
Overall explanation
This is an async button-triggered flow design. Microsoft documents that a control in a canvas app can start a flow, and Microsoft also shows that added flows are referenced from a behavior formula by using the .Run() syntax. The snippet is therefore a standard “button starts flow” implementation pattern from a canvas app.



It is also asynchronous in character, not transactional. Microsoft states that cloud flows are inherently asynchronous, and separately notes that flows started from a canvas app can continue even if Power Apps is closed. That combination is what makes this pattern useful for offloaded business logic after a user action.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• B. In-transaction server logic describes the Dataverse plug-in style of design, not a canvas app calling .Run() on a cloud flow. Microsoft explicitly distinguishes plug-ins as the mechanism that runs in the Dataverse transaction, while Power Automate is described as asynchronous workflow. This snippet is not showing a transaction-bound enforcement point.

• C. Triggerless app reference model is incorrect because Microsoft requires flows added to a canvas app to have a Power Apps trigger. The app-side .Run() reference is not enough by itself; the flow still has to be built as a Power Apps-triggered flow to be added and used properly. This option sounds plausible, but it contradicts the documented requirements for adding existing flows.

• D. Label Text invocation pattern is wrong because Microsoft’s examples place the flow reference in a behavior formula such as a button’s OnSelect. The snippet is a behavior-driven submission pattern, not something intended for a nonbehavior display property. The question is testing recognition of the app action pattern, not UI text rendering.

