Overall explanation
A PCF code component is the best redesign because the current script is being used to drive reusable UI behavior rather than simple form orchestration. The snippet depends on DOM manipulation, which is brittle as a long-term reusable pattern. A PCF control gives the team a supported component model for rendering and behavior, along with properties, lifecycle hooks, and solution packaging.



The important design distinction is that client scripting is best for form events and page logic, while reusable custom UI should generally move into a component model. A shared JavaScript library may centralize the code, but it would still preserve the same weak architectural pattern around DOM coupling. A business rule is too limited for this kind of custom rendered UI, and an HTML web resource is still a less modern and less integrated reusable control strategy than PCF.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Business rule visibility logic. Business rules are useful for straightforward conditional logic such as requiredness, visibility, or basic data-driven behavior. They are not the right answer when the requirement is a reusable custom UI artifact with controlled rendering. This option also does not address the fact that the current design is manipulating a custom visual element rather than simply toggling a supported field property. It solves a narrower class of problems than the one presented.



• B. Shared JavaScript helper library. Centralizing the library would improve duplication, but it would not fix the architectural weakness of using client script plus DOM access as the reusable UI strategy. The same brittleness would remain, and the implementation would still be tied to page structure assumptions. That means the team would standardize an unstable design instead of correcting it. It is better than copying scripts, but it is not the best redesign.



• D. Hosted HTML web resource. An HTML web resource can host custom UI and might look attractive as a reusable visual artifact. However, it still requires more manual hosting and integration patterns than a PCF control, and it is not as natural a fit for a reusable Power Apps component strategy. It also does not provide the same first-class component model for modern control design. For standardized reusable UI behavior, PCF is the stronger answer.

