• A. Edit managed components downstream. Microsoft explicitly warns against making changes directly to components that are part of a managed solution. Doing so creates an unmanaged layer on top of the managed component. That unmanaged layer can override later updates, create inconsistencies between environments, and complicate uninstall or upgrade behavior.



• C. Add full table assets by default. This sounds convenient, but Microsoft’s dependency guidance says adding unnecessary elements can increase solution complexity, apply multiple managed layers to components, and create conflicts during updates. It is better to include only the parts of the component that the solution actually uses. Over-including assets makes layer management worse, not better.



• D. Rely on Active layer fixes. Depending on the Active layer is precisely what causes many downstream problems in managed environments. Microsoft notes that unmanaged customizations at the top layer determine runtime behavior and can prevent imported managed changes from becoming effective. That makes Active-layer fixes a symptom of poor ALM discipline, not a recommended operating model.

